Date: Thu, 28 Jan 1993 17:48:19 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: DIET: Too much of everything

> Me:
> > The most longevous diet, in my estimation, is low in all three of
> > fats, carbohydrates, and protein in that order of importance.   

Perry Meztger:
> Whoa. That leaves NOTHING to eat. 

Well no, it leaves less.  A nearly sendentary person my size (6'1",
220) can usually get by on 1,500-2,500 calories per day, compared
to the U.S. mean of 3,000+.  Dr. Roy Walford recommends the low 
end of that range.  If a person is getting 30% of their
calories from fat and eliminated their entire fat intake, that
wouldn't be nearly enough reduction in calories.  Unless the
diet is very high in protein relative to carbs and fat, it
will be used to build protein or passed through the kidneys,
rather than being burned for energy.

That means all three sources of calories can can go lower.  Weight 
loss doesn't continue because the metabolism slows way down (being 
on a diet I can garuntee you this is true. :-)  Note the many studies 
of up to 50% increase in rodent lifespan with greatly reduced calorie 
intake.  This is lost if one makes up for the lowered fat and protein
with increase carbohydrate consumption.

Further, many carbohydrates, both starches and sugars have a
high glycemic index which may stress the body, putting it at
risk of diabetes, atherosclerosis, etc.  Refining of sugars
and grains has nothing to do with this problem; the brown stuff
has the same glycemic index.   Starches are converted to
glucose in the mouth and stomach; their effect on blood sugar
is in the same range as that of table sugar (sucrose).

So, I conclude that compared to the average, all three of fat,
carbohydrates and protein can be reduced, and the fat reduction
should be largest of all.  Despite the nostrums about fat as
a percent of calories, it's probably total fat intake that counts;
certainly one would not benefit by increasing carbs instead of
reducing fat!

> Admittedly fat and refined sugars aren't great for you, but going out
> and eating bran seven times a day isn't likely to do you much good, either.

"Bran seven times a day?"  Sounds like a great stereotype for
strict vegans. :-)  Fiber comes from vegetables, whole grains, 
fruits, etc. as well as concentrated brans.  It benefits the digestive 
track and helps induce satiety.  Although at least Eric Raymond claims 
to still be hungry with a gut full of bulk veggies, I find it very 
satisfying even when the calorie amount is far less.  To fiber I would 
add other undigestable bulk materials, and non-carcinogenic spices for 
their anti-oxiditaive effect, to induce satiety, and to keep sin-less 
eating enjoyable.  Artificial sweeteners can also be useful for 
enjoyment and satiety.

